  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The analysis is restricted to officially designated working days, meaning the identified scaling laws and built-environment relationships may not apply to weekends or holidays.",
      "what": "This study analyzes mobility-derived urban vitality data across 30 Chinese cities over 25 working days to identify spatiotemporal scaling laws at both grid and concentric-ring scales. It measures how vitality fluctuations relate to built-environment factors across residential, employment, public-service, and transportation land-use categories, mapping their nonlinear associations with temporal scaling exponents.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that urban vitality scaling laws are not uniform but vary systematically by land-use type, revealing distinct temporal persistence and spatial attenuation patterns. This provides a framework for understanding how specific urban functions and built-environment thresholds shape human activity dynamics."
    }
